From e7d6be5f8acce407dd0b312b9d67629dbc251fcb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Michal Frdlik <michaelf.ms@gmail.com>
Date: Mon, 27 Jul 2026 06:44:31 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Thylacine CL-4c: prepend the multicall tool name only
 when the exec path needs it

The llvm multicall driver dispatches on argv[0], so a sub-invocation whose
executable does not name the wanted tool must be told which to become; that is
Driver::PrependArg. clang set it whenever `NeedsPrependArg || CanonicalPrefixes`,
and CanonicalPrefixes defaults on, so a directly-invoked tool got PrependArg =
filename(argv0) even though NeedsPrependArg was false. The comment justified the
disjunct with "PrependArg will be null so setPrependArg will be a no-op" -- true
only for non-multicall builds, where main() passes a null PrependArg. In a
multicall build llvm-driver.cpp's MakeDriverArgs always supplies a name.

That is harmless when the install uses symlinks, because getMainExecutable
canonicalises bin/clang++ back to bin/llvm and the child really does need the
name. It is wrong when the tool is a plain copy, or on a target where
getMainExecutable cannot canonicalise at all and returns argv0: Path already
names the tool, and Command::Execute emits [Path, "clang++", "-cc1", ...]. The
cc1 child then fails clang_main's `Args[1] == "-cc1"` test, re-enters as a
driver, and rejects "-cc1" as an unknown argument. The integrated path breaks
identically, since ExecuteCC1Tool also reads ArgV[1].

Prepend only when what we will exec does not already name the tool we need it to
be. Symlink installs and `llvm clang++ ...` are unaffected; copy installs and
canonicalisation-less targets now work. Thylacine is the latter on both counts:
it has no symlinks, so /clade/bin/clang++ is a copy, and no /proc/self/exe.
---
 clang/tools/driver/driver.cpp | 24 ++++++++++++++++++------
 1 file changed, 18 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

diff --git a/clang/tools/driver/driver.cpp b/clang/tools/driver/driver.cpp
index 490136961..7d2d74c10 100644
--- a/clang/tools/driver/driver.cpp
+++ b/clang/tools/driver/driver.cpp
@@ -358,12 +358,24 @@ int clang_main(int Argc, char **Argv, const llvm::ToolContext &ToolContext) {
                    /*Title=*/"clang LLVM compiler", VFS);
   auto TargetAndMode = ToolChain::getTargetAndModeFromProgramName(ProgName);
   TheDriver.setTargetAndMode(TargetAndMode);
-  // If -canonical-prefixes is set, GetExecutablePath will have resolved Path
-  // to the llvm driver binary, not clang. In this case, we need to use
-  // PrependArg which should be clang-*. Checking just CanonicalPrefixes is
-  // safe even in the normal case because PrependArg will be null so
-  // setPrependArg will be a no-op.
-  if (ToolContext.NeedsPrependArg || CanonicalPrefixes)
+  // PrependArg tells a sub-invocation which tool of the llvm multicall driver
+  // to become. It is needed exactly when the executable we will re-invoke
+  // (Path) does not itself name this tool: with -canonical-prefixes,
+  // GetExecutablePath resolves a tool-named *symlink* back to the llvm driver
+  // binary, so the child would dispatch on "llvm" and must be told the tool.
+  //
+  // When the tool is instead installed as a plain copy -- or on a target where
+  // getMainExecutable cannot canonicalise at all (no /proc/self/exe), so Path
+  // is just argv0 -- Path already names the tool and the child re-dispatches on
+  // argv[0]. Prepending there is not merely redundant but wrong: it shifts
+  // -cc1 to argv[2], the cc1 child fails clang_main's `Args[1] == "-cc1"` test
+  // and re-enters as a driver, and rejects its own -cc1 arguments. (The same
+  // shift breaks the in-process path, whose ExecuteCC1Tool reads ArgV[1] too.)
+  // So compare what we will exec against the tool we need it to be.
+  if (ToolContext.NeedsPrependArg ||
+      (CanonicalPrefixes && ToolContext.PrependArg &&
+       llvm::sys::path::filename(Path) !=
+           llvm::sys::path::filename(ToolContext.PrependArg)))
     TheDriver.setPrependArg(ToolContext.PrependArg);
 
   insertTargetAndModeArgs(TargetAndMode, Args, SavedStrings);
-- 
